+Where an \code{enter} answered no or pending, the graph tracks as \emph{refused}
+what the read would have looked at there: a member's slot of the next name,
+where a walk tried it; what a read would have read of the node it acts on, or of
+a member it hands over; the slot of the name in the directory that ended a
+lookup's search. The nodes an answer depended on are tracked as refused too
+(\S\ref{sec:running}). Not refused: a member passed over for lacking the name;
+what a refused read and another both tracked.
+
+A change marks:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item a write: the readers of the node's value;
+\item a placement or unplacement: those of that slot or its node's whole
+  directory, or whose walk looked in that slot;
+\item a move: those its placement and unplacement would;
+\item one making an id's node here, or not here: as a change to every slot
+  holding that id would;
+\item letting go: \S\ref{sec:ephemeral}.
+\end{itemize}
+A change marks even when it leaves things as they were (as an empty patch list
+does); whether an equal value matters is the reader's call. It marks the reader
+it was made with as any other, and names its process, so a process can pass over
+its own changes. \emph{Only through refused reads}: all it marked a reader
+through was refused; what a scheduler does then: \code{SCHEDULER.tex} (Marks).
 
